Power analysis yields MDE $\beta_{\mathrm{std}} = 0.14$ at 80\% power (decreasing to 0.048 at $K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:power_analysis}). The bootstrap attenuation at $K{=}3$ (OLS 12/12 $\to$ bootstrap 4/12) is consistent with this MDE, though positive coefficient direction is robust across all methods. Random subsampling further confirms stability (Appendix~\ref{app:random_subsample}).

\subsection{Panel-Size-Dependent Predictor Shift}
\label{sec:cross_k}

Table~\ref{tab:method_comparison} reveals that the $K{=}3$ pattern is method-sensitive: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance ranges from 0/12 (clustered SE) to 12/12 (OLS), with fractional logit giving 9/12. This contrasts with $K{\geq}5$, where $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s dominance is consistent across all methods (bootstrap 7/12 at $K{=}5$; fractional logit 11/12).

We repeat the wild cluster bootstrap at $K \in \{3, 5, 7\}$, constructing all $\binom{15}{K}$ panels with $K$-position max-$p$ correction.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_regime_transition.pdf}
\caption{Predictor shift across panel sizes. $\eta_{\max}$ significance (orange) declines from 3/12 to 0/12, while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance (blue) rises from 4/12 to 7/12 as $K$ increases from 3 to 5. Shaded region marks the predictor shift zone $K^* \in (3, 5)$. Bootstrap: $B{=}9999$, Rademacher weights, position-permutation max-$p$, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$.}
\label{fig:regime_transition}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:regime_transition} reveals a predictor shift between $K{=}3$ and $K{=}5$. At $K{=}3$, both predictors contribute modestly ($\eta_{\max}$ 3/12, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ 4/12)---co-determination. At $K{=}5$, $\eta_{\max}$ drops to 0/12 while $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ rises to 7/12, all positive. Restricting to the four $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$-stable attacks ($\rho{\geq}0.91$; Appendix~\ref{app:attacked_keff}) confirms that the signal is not driven by measurement-unstable conditions. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significance increases monotonically ($4 \to 7 \to 9$) from $K{=}3$ to $K{=}7$ (\S\ref{sec:analysis}).

\subsection{Random vs.\ Adaptive Fault Contrast}
\label{sec:rf_contrast}

We simulate non-adversarial noise by randomly flipping judge votes at observed per-judge error rates. Random faults yield $R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$ ($K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ significant 2/2); adaptive attack reduces predictability ($R^2{=}0.275$ at $K{=}5$) but $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ retains power (7/12). The predictability gap narrows from ${\sim}0.6$ ($K{=}3$) to $0.43$ ($K{=}5$).

\subsection{Uniform Positive Direction Across Attack Types}
\label{sec:gam_taxonomy}

GAM bootstrap confirms that $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient direction is positive in all 12 conditions (88.9--100\% positive rate at $K{=}3$, 100\% at $K{=}5$; Figure~\ref{fig:gam_split}). A Kruskal--Wallis test finds no significant magnitude difference between targeted and shared-bias attacks ($p{=}0.200$ at $K{=}3$, $p{=}0.423$ at $K{=}5$).

\subsection{Aggregation Rules and the Diversity Channel}
\label{sec:aggregation}

Table~\ref{tab:aggregation} reports regression results for four aggregation methods across all 12 conditions.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}

\section{Introduction}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_framework.pdf}
\caption{Overview of our evaluation framework. Given $N$ candidate LLM judges, we construct $K$-judge panels (\S\ref{sec:experiments}), subject them to six adversarial attack types (\S\ref{sec:threat_model}), and assess vulnerability through two complementary metrics: $\eta_{\max}$ (weakest-link individual susceptibility) and $K_{\text{eff}}$ (effective independent channels via mutual information). Panel decisions are aggregated through four methods of increasing sophistication (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}).}
\label{fig:framework}
\end{figure*}

Multi-judge panels have emerged as the dominant defense against bias and manipulation in LLM-based evaluation. After \citet{zheng2023judging} demonstrated that individual LLM judges can rival human annotators, subsequent work exposed systematic vulnerabilities: position bias, verbosity preferences, and sycophancy compromise the reliability of any single judge \citep{wang2023fair}. The natural response has been to aggregate decisions across diverse models, drawing inspiration from Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT), where majority voting among independent nodes tolerates faulty responses \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical}. A recent systematization of LLM-judge security reports that 7-model committees reduce attack success rates from 50--68\% to 10--19\% \citep{almasoud2026security}. Diversity, by this logic, is the key ingredient for secure evaluation.

Yet the evidence for diversity as a causal defense is weaker than it appears. The observed ASR reduction conflates panel diversity (judge independence) with individual robustness (strongest members). No existing work disentangles these across panel sizes. BFT adversaries control faulty nodes internally, whereas evaluation adversaries manipulate inputs externally; the SDPI bounds joint failure but assumes non-adaptive faults \citep{schneier2000secrets}.

In our experiments, we observe a panel-size-dependent predictor shift. The positive direction of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient across all 12 conditions (FDR-significant under OLS; GAM bootstrap confirms 88.9--100\% positive rate) is directionally consistent with adversarial ensemble theory \citep{goodfellow2015adversarial,papernot2016transferability}, but the $K$-dependent transition in predictor dominance is previously uncharacterized. At $K{=}3$, both $\eta_{\max}$ and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ show some significance; at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the sole significant predictor while $\eta_{\max}$ drops to zero significance. This transition arises in the LLM judge setting, where adversaries manipulate through natural language rather than gradient-based perturbations, and aggregation rules provide a practical modulation lever. GAM bootstrap confirms no significant difference between attack types (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$).

Controlled experiments across 15 models, $K \in \{3,5,7\}$, 6 attacks, and 2 benchmarks confirm this shift (Figure~\ref{fig:framework}). Under random faults, panel composition is highly predictive ($R^2 = 0.73$--$0.81$); the predictability collapse under adaptive attack is most pronounced at $K{=}3$. Aggregation rules influence predictability ($R^2$: threshold 0.83, Bayesian 0.09); Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12 under OLS).

These findings yield three contributions:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Diversity--vulnerability association.} $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s coefficient is uniformly positive across all 12 conditions (OLS and GAM bootstrap). The diversity--capability confound ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$) limits causal interpretation: Cinelli--Hazlett robustness values do not exceed the confound in 78\% of conditions (28/36), though fractional logit confirms the positive association ($K{=}3$: 9/12; $K{=}5$: 11/12; $K{=}7$: 12/12). Significance is method-dependent (OLS 12/12; bootstrap 4/12 at $K{=}3$); no attack-type split exists (Kruskal--Wallis $p{=}0.200$; \S\ref{sec:dominance}, \S\ref{sec:gam_taxonomy}).
\item \textbf{Panel-size-dependent predictor shift.} At $K{=}3$, both $\eta_{\max}$ (3/12) and $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (4/12) contribute under bootstrap (co-determination); at $K{\geq}5$, $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ becomes the sole significant predictor (7/12 at $K{=}5$, 9/12 at $K{=}7$) while $\eta_{\max}$ drops to 0/12 (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}, \S\ref{sec:rf_contrast}).
\item \textbf{Aggregation as predictability control.} Aggregation rules influence how much variance panel composition explains ($R^2$: threshold 0.83 $\to$ Bayesian 0.09). Bayesian calibration attenuates $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (5+/1$-$) but $\eta_{\max}$ persists (9/12), providing partial rather than complete defense (\S\ref{sec:aggregation}, \S\ref{sec:analysis}).
\end{enumerate}

Our findings describe descriptive associations; causal interpretation is fundamentally limited by the diversity--capability collinearity ($r{=}0.70$--$0.81$, VIF${\approx}2.0$--$2.9$; \S\ref{sec:analysis}).

