\section{Related Work}

\paragraph{LLM-as-Judge and Multi-Judge Evaluation.}
LLM judges gained traction after \citet{zheng2023judging} established them as scalable alternatives to human annotation. \citet{wang2023fair} documented systematic failure modes (position bias, verbosity, sycophancy), motivating multi-judge panels; \citet{raina2024judgerobust} quantified robustness gaps under universal adversarial attacks, \citet{maloyan2025investigating} demonstrated prompt injection vulnerabilities in LLM-as-a-judge systems, and \citet{stureborg2024inconsistent} and \citet{ye2024justice} further catalogued inconsistency and bias in LLM evaluators. Aggregating noisy annotators traces back to \citet{dawid1979maximum}; \citet{verga2024replacing} showed diverse panels can match single large judges, and \citet{panickssery2024llm} identified self-preference bias driving within-family correlations. These frameworks assume benign conditions; we test whether aggregation benefits survive adversarial manipulation. \citet{almasoud2026security} reported that 7-model committees reduce ASR from 50--68\% to 10--19\%, but did not disentangle diversity from individual robustness. \citet{tamas2025benchmarking} benchmark adversarial risks in multi-agent LLM systems but focus on agent coordination rather than judge panel composition.

\paragraph{Condorcet Jury Theorem.}
\citet{condorcet1785essai} proved that majority voting accuracy converges to 1 when per-voter accuracy exceeds 0.5; \citet{ladha1992condorcet} extended the theorem to correlated voters, showing that positive correlation weakens the convergence guarantee. CJT explicitly assumes independent, non-adversarial errors; its failure under adaptive adversaries is trivially expected \citep{tramer2018ensemble,kariyappa2019improving,pang2019improving}. Our contribution is not this well-known direction but the \emph{panel-size-dependent structure}: the predictor shift from co-determination at $K{=}3$ to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ dominance at $K{\geq}5$.

\paragraph{Ensemble Diversity Theory.}
\citet{krogh1995neural} proved that ensemble error equals average member error minus a diversity term; \citet{brown2005managing} showed diversity reduces error only when members maintain sufficiently low bias. In our setting, weaker judges introduce bias large enough to offset diversity benefits. \citet{kuncheva2003measures} found that no pairwise diversity measure reliably predicted ensemble improvement; our $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a $K$-dependent pattern: at $K{=}3$, OLS significance (12/12) attenuates under bootstrap (4/12); at $K{\geq}5$, it survives bootstrap inference (7/12). Recent information-theoretic frameworks \citep{yang2026diversity,turkmen2026ensemble} characterize diversity under benign evaluation; we provide the adversarial complement.

\paragraph{Adversarial Robustness and Transferability.}
Adversarial vulnerability was formalized by \citet{goodfellow2015adversarial}, with transferability demonstrated by \citet{papernot2016transferability,papernot2017practical}. \citet{tramer2018ensemble} introduced ensemble adversarial training but did not eliminate cross-model transfer. Controlled diversity via uncorrelated gradients \citep{kariyappa2019improving} or diversity regularization \citep{pang2019improving} strengthens ensemble robustness in vision, but requires coordinating member training, a control absent in post-hoc LLM judge selection. Three differences motivate our empirical approach: adversaries use natural-language manipulation rather than gradient perturbations, panel decisions are discrete votes, and aggregation rules are a controllable design parameter.

\paragraph{BFT and Security Games.}
Classical BFT \citep{lamport1982byzantine,castro1999practical} tolerates up to $f < n/3$ faulty nodes but assumes non-strategic faults; \citet{schneier2000secrets} articulated the weakest-link principle that an attacker exploits the single most vulnerable component. Our framework adopts the Stackelberg structure of \citet{tambe2011security}. Correlated LLM judge errors \citep{kim2025correlated,kuai2026independent} violate BFT's independence assumption, motivating our empirical decomposition. \citet{kuai2026independent} propose the Difficulty-Weighted Behavioral Entanglement Index (DBEI), weighting agreement by item difficulty; $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ captures a complementary dimension via Gaussian copula MI, connecting directly to the SDPI bound. Three mitigation strategies address judge correlation through different mechanisms: DBEI-based reweighting, our Bayesian calibration baseline, and credibility scoring \citep{ebrahimi2025adversary}.

\paragraph{Information-Theoretic Evaluation.}
Information-theoretic approaches to LLM evaluation address statistical validity \citep{robertson2025stepwise,feng2026noisy}, single-model attack costs \citep{kaneko2025bits}, sequential selection \citep{jung2026logarithmic}, and truthfulness \citep{chen2025incentivizing,qiu2026truthfulness}, but assume honest judges or single-model adversaries. We address the remaining gap: adversarial manipulation interacting with multi-judge composition.

\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Measure} & \textbf{Sig} & \textbf{Dir.} & \textbf{$|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|$} & $\boldsymbol{\rho}$ \textbf{w/} $\boldsymbol{K_{\mathrm{eff}}}$ \\
\midrule
$Q$-statistic    & 9/12  & 6+/3$-$ & 0.251 & 0.599 \\
Disagreement     & 11/12 & 11+/0$-$ & 0.481 & 0.934 \\
$\kappa$-diversity & 11/12 & 11+/0$-$ & 0.497 & 0.978 \\
\addlinespace
$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ (ref.) & 12/12 & 12+/0$-$ & 0.430 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Comparison of diversity measures as predictors of panel ASR (OLS, FDR-corrected, 12 conditions pooled across $K$). Sig: FDR-significant conditions. $|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|$: mean absolute standardized coefficient. $\rho$: Pearson correlation with $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$.}
\label{tab:alt_diversity}
\end{table}

$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ and $\kappa$-diversity \citep{kuncheva2003measures} are near-equivalent ($\rho{=}0.978$), with comparable significance counts (12/12 vs.\ 11/12) and effect sizes (0.430 vs.\ 0.497). Disagreement rate achieves similar predictive power ($\rho{=}0.934$, 11/12). The $Q$-statistic is the weakest predictor (9/12, $|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|{=}0.251$), consistent with its known insensitivity to the type of error correlation that is associated with panel vulnerability \citep{kuncheva2003measures}. $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s advantage over these alternatives is not predictive power but interpretability: it quantifies the number of effective independent channels in the panel, connecting directly to the information-theoretic foundation of panel aggregation (\S\ref{sec:experiments}).

\section{$\kappa$-Diversity Predictor Shift Comparison}
\label{app:kappa_regime}

To test whether the predictor shift (\S\ref{sec:cross_k}) is specific to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ or a generic property of diversity measures, we repeat the wild cluster bootstrap analysis replacing $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ with $\kappa$-diversity ($1 - \bar{\kappa}$, where $\bar{\kappa}$ is the mean pairwise Cohen's $\kappa$ on clean winner labels; $\rho{=}0.978$ with $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$).

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{rcccc}
\toprule
& \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{$K_{\mathrm{eff}}$}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{$\kappa$-diversity}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \cmidrule(lr){4-5}
$\boldsymbol{K}$ & \textbf{div.\ sig} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} & \textbf{div.\ sig} & $\boldsymbol{\eta_{\max}}$ \textbf{sig} \\
\midrule
3 & 4/12  & 3/12 & 8/12 & 1/12 \\
5 & 7/12 & 0/12 & 7/12 & 4/12 \\
7 & 9/12  & 0/12 & 6/12 & 1/12 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Predictor shift comparison: $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ vs.\ $\kappa$-diversity across panel sizes (wild cluster bootstrap, BH FDR $\alpha{=}0.05$). $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ exhibits a clear shift ($4 \to 7 \to 9$); $\kappa$-diversity significance decreases monotonically ($8 \to 7 \to 6$), showing no predictor shift.}
\label{tab:kappa_regime}
\end{table}

$\kappa$-diversity is a stronger predictor than $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ at $K{=}3$ (8/12 vs.\ 4/12), consistent with its larger mean effect size ($|\bar{\beta}_{\mathrm{std}}|{=}0.497$ vs.\ 0.430; Table~\ref{tab:alt_diversity}). However, $\kappa$-diversity shows no predictor shift: its significance \emph{decreases} from $K{=}3$ to $K{=}7$ ($8 \to 7 \to 6$), the opposite of $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s pattern ($4 \to 7 \to 9$). Furthermore, under $\kappa$-diversity, $\eta_{\max}$ retains significance at $K{=}5$ (4/12), whereas under $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$ it drops to 0/12. The predictor shift---co-determination at $K{=}3$ transitioning to diversity dominance at $K{\geq}5$---is thus specific to $K_{\mathrm{eff}}$'s MI-based formulation, which captures effective judge independence rather than pairwise label agreement.

